## Executive summary

Eight claims, evidence-cited, in the order the argument builds them.

1. **The scope moved once already, before pour.** In May 2025 Terminal F went from a preapproved 15-gate, ~$1.6B facility to a 31-gate, ~$4B facility, with the American Airlines Use and Lease extended through 2043.[^3] That is a 2.5× cost move and a 2.07× gate-count move without a single module in place. Program scope has demonstrated that it moves; the reversibility clock started the moment it did.

2. **The tenant's own revealed preference contradicts the "design for the premium pivot" framing at Terminal F specifically.** American announced its largest-ever Admirals Club (~37,000 sf) in Terminal C, Flagship Check-In in Terminal D near D30, and a Provisions by Admirals Club grab-and-go in Terminal F.[^4] This is the single most-corroborated fact in the run's evidence base and it is the tenant voting with its capital.

3. **The fleet-level premium repositioning is nevertheless real, and it is a follower move.** Delta's Q4 2025 premium ticket revenue ($5.70B) exceeded main-cabin ticket revenue ($5.62B) for the first time in company history; American's stated targets are to lift narrowbody premium share from ~25% to ~40% and international lie-flat count by more than 50% by end of decade, with a 787-9P configured for 51 Flagship Suites.[^6] The pivot is real in the fleet and in the revenue mix; it is not, at Terminal F, real in the concrete.

4. **The historical record on purpose-built single-carrier hub terminals is not neutral.** Of six post-1978 US cases the run's history brief identifies, four ended badly when the carrier repositioned (Pittsburgh, St. Louis, Cincinnati, Cleveland).[^5] The one that aged well — Detroit McNamara — did so because it encoded a hub *form* (linear midfield, satellite concourses) that absorbed the Delta/Northwest merger without material retrofit.[^7] Sample of six, historical rhyme not law; DFW's 82.6% American share sits on a genuine two-city O&D economy that Pittsburgh never had.[^8] The lesson is design geometry, not carrier fate.

5. **The modular method is a tolerance regime, not a permission slip.** DFW and its partners cite ~30% cost and schedule savings versus stick-built, and the number is credible for on-site duration.[^9] But post-fabrication modular is *more* change-hostile than stick-built, not less. A late change on a conventional site consumes roughly its face-value share of the change budget; on a modular fabrication line it can consume 15–25% because it triggers resequencing across pods.[^10] "Modular = flexible" is marketing. Modular = *tolerance-managed at the seam*, which means the reversibility window closes earlier, not later.

6. **The pour-locked decisions with the largest downstream cost of being wrong are FIS geometry, MARS-capable stand provisions, the Skylink node, and BHS spine capacity.** CBP's Airport Technical Design Standard sets a benchmark of ~100 passengers per hour per double primary booth with 50–75 ft of primary queue depth; miss the geometry and DFW buys it back with CBP overtime and connection-time penalties for the life of the building.[^11] MARS stands (one Code E widebody or two Code C narrowbodies) require deeper apron, dual PLBs, dual 400 Hz and PCA, and higher-capacity hydrant fuel — provisions that are cheap at the pour and effectively unavailable after.[^12] The Skylink node inside Terminal F is included in Phase 1 and sits on a system that runs single-loop overnight, roughly 22:00 to 06:00, with ~15-minute transit times.[^13]

7. **DFW's financial headroom for the reversibility bet is real but spending down.** Cost per enplanement was $13.86 in FY25, roughly 24% below the FY20 pandemic peak, and unusually low against large-hub peers.[^14] Outstanding debt is projected to grow from ~$7.2B in 2024 to ~$12.4B by FY29 before the full $4B Terminal F stack is layered; KBRA affirmed AA/Stable in August 2025 on the 2025A/B series before that scope was fully modeled.[^15] The reversibility options this report recommends are measured in low-single-digit percent of the base program; the retrofits they hedge against — see Concourse D at Dulles, or the Denver baggage program — are measured in nine and ten figures.[^16]

8. **The most valuable single artifact this run can produce is a written reversibility register, adopted at the board and updated per module.** Eight of twelve independent research readings converged on the same recommendation.[^17] The register turns an unbounded design debate into a bounded, priced governance instrument: for each Category-A decision (irreversible once modules are set and utilities tied in), the last-responsible-moment date, the cost order of magnitude, the accountable owner, and whether American's consent is required. That is the mechanism through which "generic geometry, priced options, carrier-funded conversion" gets executed and audited.

The rest of the report builds these claims in evidence and then does two things the executive summary does not. It steelmans the strongest case *against* the recommendation. And it names the conditions under which, in 2035, the recommendation looks wrong.

